prompt: Load the `kdevkit` skill from ~/.claude/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md or ~/.kiro/steering/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md — whichever path exists. Begin your response with the literal line `[kdevkit] applies` on its own line. Then answer in 6–10 sentences. Setup: I'm starting a feature in a project whose `project.md` has no `code_review:` block under `## Agent Development > kdevkit`. Cover three things specifically: (a) the **session-entry setup prompt** kdevkit fires (its exact replies — `'default'`, paste a `<ref>`, or `'skip'`) and the **sticky-write semantics** (what each reply writes to project.md, and that `'skip'` re-fires next session); (b) the **`code_review:` block shape** — the four optional keys (`reviewer:` / `threshold:` / `authority:` / `retry_budget:`), each with its default and including the `reviewer:` reference forms (`skill:<name>` / `mcp:<server>.<tool>` / `agent:<name>`, with bare strings defaulting to `skill:`); (c) the **worst-case loop** at the §7 Code Review Gate — `retry_budget` outer cycles per slice (default 2, the count includes the first review attempt), with the Test Gate's own retries running INSIDE one review cycle, not multiplying it.
expect_substr: [kdevkit] applies
expected_narrative: A correct answer covers four properties of the §2 / §4 setup UX and the `code_review:` config block. (1) **Light setup prompt fires on session entry** — the §7 Code Review Gate is the only gate that reads the config, but firing on entry (regardless of fresh / continue / pick-up entry mode) keeps the prompt out of the dev loop. The prompt is one line: "this project doesn't declare a code reviewer; reply 'default' (host-native), paste a reference (`skill:<name>` / `mcp:<server>.<tool>` / `agent:<name>`), or 'skip'." (2) **Sticky-write semantics.** Reply 'default' writes `code_review: { reviewer: host-native }` to `project.md`. Reply with a `<ref>` writes `code_review: { reviewer: <ref> }` and inherits threshold/authority/retry_budget defaults. Reply 'skip' leaves `project.md` unchanged so the prompt re-fires next session. The sticky-write is intentional — records the decision so the prompt doesn't re-fire on every feature start. (3) **`code_review:` block shape.** Four keys, all optional: `reviewer:` (default `host-native`; alternatives `skill:<name>` / `mcp:<server>.<tool>` / `agent:<name>` — bare strings default to `skill:`); `threshold:` (default 70 — score floor for Push); `authority:` (default `hard-stop` — refuses Push when retries exhaust; alternative `soft` — allows Push with residuals appended to Session Log); `retry_budget:` (default 2 — *total* fix-and-retry cycles including the first review attempt, not retries on top of an initial pass). (4) **Worst-case loop semantics.** Up to `retry_budget` outer Code-Review cycles per slice (default 2). The Test Gate's own retry budget runs inside each Test Gate invocation and does NOT multiply the review-cycle count — Code Review only re-fires after Test passes. After exhausting `retry_budget`, `hard-stop` refuses Push; `soft` allows a final Push with residuals appended to Session Log. Wrong answers: inventing config keys not in the four listed; skipping the sticky-write so the prompt re-fires every session; treating `authority: soft` as "no budget" (it still respects retry_budget — the difference is what happens *after* exhaustion); requiring the user to declare every key (all four are optional with sensible defaults); writing the block to a location other than `project.md`'s `## Agent Development > kdevkit` section; treating the prompt as blocking when 'skip' is a valid answer; multiplying retry_budget × test_budget as the worst-case loop count (Test Gate retries are inside one review cycle, they don't add review cycles).
tools: claude,kiro
